/** Manual English overrides for siman 445 — biur chametz */
export const RETRANSLATE = {
  "ateret-zekenim/1:א":
    "The custom is to burn the chametz on the eve of the 14th (Hoshana Rabbah), and some throw it into the oven when baking matzot — both are words of the Living God (Maharshal in this seif in his gloss).",

  "ateret-zekenim/1:ב":
    "But if one wants to burn immediately, etc. — it appears to me that nevertheless tomorrow he fulfills the mitzvah of biur through burning, like that chametz left on the 14th, or he burns the utensil for two reasons: one, to fulfill the mitzvah of biur on the 14th itself, which is primary; and also so people will be careful afterward not to eat chametz. If he burned before the sixth hour, it is permitted to benefit from the coals. The Tur concludes that nevertheless it is good to make a bonfire by itself, and one should not deviate from custom; especially where there is no zagir (watchman), one burns chametz in the courtyard and not in the house so as not to benefit. They are accustomed to burn even in the fifth hour like R' Yehudah, and also to be stringent like the Sages that ash is forbidden, for we take here and there stringently — so writes Maharshal. If he cooked or baked that bread and that dish, they are forbidden in benefit, and even the coals are forbidden in benefit; and even though we hold that for all burned items ash is permitted, nevertheless since it was after the sixth hour, the ash is also forbidden in benefit (Beit Yosef and the Rosh).",

  "baer-heitev/1:א":
    "He crumbles it; and if he throws it into the privy, he need not crumble. If he has collected water containing fish, he must not throw it there, for he benefits from it. Magen Avraham, Chok Yaakov. It is forbidden in all melachot when the time of burning chametz arrives until he burns, as written in siman 431. And on what Magen Avraham wrote on the Rambam — Yad Efraim challenged him and agreed with Chok Yaakov — see there.",

  "baer-heitev/1:ב":
    "The choice is his. Maharil wrote to burn specifically by day so he will remember to nullify. Maharshal received from his teachers to burn chametz on Hoshana Rabbah (and in such a case one may kindle the oven with the lulav) — see there.",

  "baer-heitev/2:א":
    "To a non-Jew. Bach wrote in a responsum siman 147 that it is forbidden to give to his male or female gentile slave chametz remaining on the 14th if the chametz is abundant so it cannot be eaten before its prohibition time, since their sustenance is upon him — it is like paying his debt with items forbidden in benefit — see there.",

  "baer-heitev/2:ב":
    "Oven. Beit Yosef wrote in Yoreh De'ah siman 142 that the flame of chametz is permitted, for it is no worse than wood of a non-Jew whose ash is forbidden yet the flame is permitted; so too for chametz — even though its ash is forbidden, nevertheless its flame is permitted — see there. Chok Yaakov challenged him and ruled the flame is forbidden — see there.",

  "baer-heitev/2:ג":
    "Forbidden in benefit. Acharonim agree to forbid even when there is no flame opposite it, only if it was cooked or baked near coals or in oven heat; also clear from Acharonim that it has no remedy by casting benefit to the Dead Sea, but one may sell to a non-Jew except for the value of the prohibition in it. Shach in Yoreh De'ah siman 110 permits only on condition he not return and sell to a Jew; so too Peri Chadash. But Magen Avraham wrote it has no remedy at all — see there; Chok Yaakov agreed with Shach when there is no flame opposite, for it is only a d'rabbanan prohibition — see there. Magen Avraham wrote even if permitted wood were also here it does not help, even though it is this-and-that cause — see there.",

  "beer-hagolah/1:א": "Mishnah Pesachim 21, and like the Sages.",
  "beer-hagolah/1:ב": "Rambam chapter 3, and like R' Yosef.",
  "beer-hagolah/2:א": "Mishnah there 24, and in the Gemara there.",
  "beer-hagolah/2:ב":
    "Words of the Rambam in chapter 3 from the mishnah and from the conclusion of the Gemara 26, and stringently.",
  "beer-hagolah/3:_": "Hagahot Maimoniyot, chapter 3.",

  "beur-hagra/1:א":
    "Seif 1 — he burns it. Rambam and the girsa also \"even crumbles,\" and even in a text that does not read \"even\" — nevertheless he explains \"even crumbles\" from that R' Yehudah says only burning; and furthermore it is impossible to say that all mishnayot in the first chapter and all baraitot where we learned they burn — all according to R' Yehudah: R' Meir, R' Yosei, R' Shimon, Rabban Gamliel, R' Eliezer, R' Yehoshua, R' Eliezer ish Bartuta — all like R' Yehudah; rather even for the Sages the essence is burning. There 21b: \"and he shall not kindle,\" etc. — it fits only for R' Yehudah, etc. 40 — for the rabbis too it works; see Tosafot s.v. behadi, etc.",

  "beur-hagra/1:ב":
    "Or throws it into the sea, and if. He explains like R' Yosef, for he said to him R' Yehudah, and R' Yehudah said — learn from this that Rabbah's statement does not stand; and so is the sugya in many places.",

  "beur-hagra/1:ג":
    "The chametz is hard. Such as wheat, as stated in the Gemara; he explains one crumbles the wheat — not like Rashi.",

  "beur-hagra/1:ד":
    "And the custom. Meaning, even for R' Yehudah burning is not required until after six, as stated 12b, and as Rabbenu Tam explains in Tosafot s.v. eimatai — R' Yehudah's reason is he derives from notar, as stated 27b; and that is after its prohibition. So too in Yerushalmi chapter 2 end law 1: \"Until its biur time has not arrived, you destroy it in 24 ways; once its biur time arrives, you destroy it by burning\" — and it follows R' Yehudah. If so, even for poskim like R' Yehudah, because the mishnah in Temurah follows him, as stated in the Gemara end Temurah — see there; and even though it was rejected, nevertheless for the rabbis' view it was not rejected, as Tosafot 27b s.v. ein and s.v. shaf explain. Nevertheless, since they burn before prohibition time, from the Torah it is only from custom; and even though the Rosh wrote that even for Rashi who explains the opposite that at six they burn for R' Yehudah, nevertheless before six in the fifth and before that R' Yehudah agrees burning is not required, rather even if he sells and gives to a non-Jew it is permitted, as stated in the first chapter 21 — and as stated 2b, the Tur already challenged from what Rashi wrote 27b s.v. she'im, etc.; and even though we say, etc. — the custom is according to Rashi's view, but the essence is as Tosafot 12b s.v. eimatai explain; and he wrote so in the Yerushalmi, and all poskim agreed.",

  "beur-hagra/1:ה":
    "And good to burn. For R' Yehudah we derive from notar as above; in Yerushalmi Shabbat chapter 2, for the view that derives from notar — one does not burn on Yom Tov, and likewise at night; and 27b is a general stringency that we derive only after prohibition time — this is what \"but,\" etc. means; see Rashi 6a s.v. oseh. At night, etc., and siman 446 seif 1 — one may say he wrote per the rabbis, but for R' Yehudah one burns by day.",

  "beur-hagra/1:ו":
    "If he wants. So they inferred from what he leaves, etc. — that what he finds he burns immediately.",

  "beur-hagra/2:א":
    "Seif 2 — and if he burned before, etc. His time passed, etc., and he shall not kindle — behold, before his time it is permitted; and in the Gemara: if he charred it before its time, it is permitted in benefit after its time.",

  "beur-hagra/2:ג":
    "And if he cooked or baked, etc. Rambam's wording; and from that he taught 40 — even on coals and even glowing coals; and so stam in chapter 7 of laws of orlah regarding orlah; but in chapter 16 laws of forbidden foods regarding orlah on coals — permitted even if they smolder — as the Rosh wrote in chapter 21 specifically for orlah and kilayim whose ash is permitted and so coals; but for chametz whose ash is forbidden, all the more coals — even if he cooked on glowing coals it is forbidden. Tosafot there s.v. bishlah and there 21b s.v. behadi, etc.; and in asherah whose ash was forbidden as stated, except asherah wood; and they said end Yom Tov coals of orlah are forbidden — not like what is written there, and there specifically with flame opposite; so Beit Yosef Yoreh De'ah 142 and Bach there; Shach there challenged them at length and his words are very correct — not like Magen Avraham here who followed the Kesef Mishneh; therefore the question was raised for Rami bar Chama on wood of hekdesh for the rabbis, and not raised for R' Yehudah on coals, since hekdesh ash is forbidden as stated there — unlike the rabbis, for there is no enhancement of wood in bread even with flame opposite. What Magen Avraham wrote — and even though chametz, etc. — nevertheless it is zuz gavra, etc. — he did not look in the Ran chapter 21 who wrote: and even though zuz gavra is permitted, etc. — we say there is enhancement of wood in bread and it is stricter than zuz gavra because the prohibition is recognizable in the permitted; and what Magen Avraham wrote to forbid for another reason — since forbidden in benefit like orlah wood for the rabbis — that is not so, for in hekdesh the reason is not because of benefit but because it is stringent; see Tosafot there s.v. hekdesh, etc., \"one might say,\" etc., meaning unlike when he kindles where the prohibition is consumed and nullified as Yom Tov 4b — not like Magen Avraham; and so here they are not plausible.",

  "beur-hagra/2:ד":
    "And likewise the coals. Since he charred it after its time as above, and even its ash is forbidden as stated end Temurah as above, and all the more coals which are stricter than ash as Tosafot s.v. bishelah.",

  "beur-hagra/3:ב":
    "And if he found it. As stated above, the halacha is not so; and so ruled Taz here.",

  "biur-halacha/2:א":
    "Forbidden in benefit — see in Mishnah Berurah, and know that although we ruled stringently even b'dieved if baked in an oven on chametz coals and ash because Acharonim ruled stringently, Shach's view in Yoreh De'ah 142 is not so — he holds there that although baking or cooking on coals alone is certainly forbidden, nevertheless baking inside an oven kindled on coals is permitted as this-and-that cause, and we do not say it is only one cause except when baking opposite the fire of the chametz body which is significant, and therefore we disregard the oven; unlike coals and ash alone. Possibly, and all the more if all coals and ash were removed and then he baked — certainly one need not be stringent b'dieved. So Magen Avraham at the beginning of s.k. 5 (and even though he later retracted and forbade this-and-that cause in chametz, many Acharonim rejected his view, and the Gra disagrees). One who examines Rabbeinu Manoach's novellae on the Rambam will see his view permits even on coals themselves b'dieved; nevertheless in practice it is hard to be lenient after all Acharonim ruled stringently, and the Gra in his commentary holds no distinction between cooking on chametz coals and ash versus cooking on them inside an oven kindled in every manner — forbidden. However, if he kindled the oven with chametz and also with permitted wood together and then baked bread there, it is literally this-and-that cause; although Taz there in Yoreh De'ah and Magen Avraham at the end of his words forbid this-and-that cause, nevertheless Shulchan Aruch and Shach there on orlah are lenient, and so in our case regarding chametz; and many Acharonim and the Gra ruled leniently on this-and-that cause, as I wrote above. Therefore in a pressing situation where he has nothing to eat or there is great loss, one may rely to be lenient — so Gra Zal.",

  "biur-halacha/2:ב":
    "And likewise the coals — regarding the flame: since it is attached to the coal, it is like the coal, as the Rashba and Rosh proved in Beitzah 39 — see there; so Taz in Yoreh De'ah siman 142. If it is not attached to the coal, Chok Yaakov is stringent for chametz, as we are stringent in the Gemara for hekdesh lest one come to use the coal itself; Chak Yosef rejected this based on Pesachim 4a: he goes after it to burn it — a dog eats it — and there is no room to reject Chok Yaakov's words, for here one in any case wants to benefit from its flame, and properly one should fear he will benefit from the coal; it is simple. Therefore it is forbidden to warm oneself by the flame or light a lamp from it and similar benefits.",

  "chok-yaakov/1:א":
    "He burns it or crumbles and casts to the wind — this is the dispute in the mishnah at the beginning of chapter Kol Sha'ah: R' Yehudah says biur of chametz is only by burning; the Sages say also crumbling and casting to the wind or throwing into the sea. The Rambam ruled like the Mechaber's wording, like the Sages; and even though the Rambam ruled end Hilchot Pesulei HaMukdashim like the Sages end Temurah that all buried items (meaning cast to wind or thrown to sea are in the category of buried, as the Tur explains) should not be burned — one must say specifically those listed in the mishnah as buried only, unlike chametz where the Sages explicitly teach both burning and burying; this is not in the general category of buried items, therefore the Rambam did not count chametz among buried or among burned. So too in responsum Emunat Shmuel siman 55. Do not challenge: since buried items may not be burned lest one benefit from ash while burned items' ash is permitted — chametz coals and ash are forbidden like buried items, so how may one burn? Answer: because of chametz's stringency one should be stringent in burning so one not come to violate \"it shall not be seen\" and \"it shall not be found,\" even though lenient in ash. Or: since all destroy as required before six (as siman 434), then coals and ash are permitted unless they did not destroy afterward — an uncommon case, so the Sages did not decree. This is my understanding of the Rambam; R' Yosef and the Rosh, though they have another girsa in the Sages' words without \"even,\" nevertheless hold burning is permitted even for the Sages, as the sugya there explains for the Sages: their reason is lest he not find wood to burn and sit idle, and the Torah said \"you shall destroy\"; the Rosh explicitly ruled in chapter Kol Sha'ah that all buried items — if one wants to be stringent and burn, he may, like R' Yehudah end Temurah. The reason: since the mishnah's stam is stringent, we establish Temurah like R' Yehudah; this is why our custom burns chametz found, even for poskim like R' Yehudah who hold biur is only burning (see Beit Yosef Yoreh De'ah 294 and Orach Chayim Klal 61 din 13). I expanded on this because I saw Magen Avraham forced the sugya like the Rambam very far from Rif and Rosh — the matter is clear as stated.",

  "chok-yaakov/1:ב":
    "Hard chametz — likewise if wheat were in a sack, scatter from the sack and throw to the sea; but one need not grind to crumble; so Rashi and poskim. Magen Avraham wrote the Maggid Mishneh implies for the Rambam one must also crumble the wheat itself — his words are not compelling; I never saw such custom. He also wrote if throwing into the privy, no crumbling needed, and we do not decree lest an animal eat it — unlike water with fish where throwing is forbidden. Orach Chayim Klal 61 din 13: they pour meat-and-milk stew into the privy or river, which is like burning since no creature can benefit — even without a dog there.",

  "chok-yaakov/1:ג":
    "Good to burn by day — meaning before the sixth hour. Maharshal wrote in responsum 26 to burn on Hoshana Rabbah; so Mahari in siman 153 that one may kindle the oven with the lulav; Mahari was accustomed to use it to kindle the oven mouth when baking matzot — see siman 75.",

  "chok-yaakov/1:ד":
    "The choice is in his hand. In responsum Mahari Weil siman 170 he wrote: nevertheless he fulfills \"you shall destroy\" in its time, since at biur time he has no chametz in his house even if he destroyed thirty days before Pesach — end quote. And if he gave to a non-Jew, etc. (Bach responsum 147: forbidden to give to gentile slave remaining chametz on the 14th if abundant and cannot be eaten before prohibition, since sustenance is on him — like paying debt with forbidden benefit — end quote).",

  "chok-yaakov/2:ב":
    "He shall not kindle with it. Beit Yosef Yoreh De'ah 142 s.k. 1 wrote the flame of chametz is permitted, no worse than non-Jewish wood whose ash is forbidden yet flame permitted; so for chametz — ash forbidden, flame permitted — this seems simple — end quote. I did not delve into his view, for it is plainly the opposite in Beitzah 39: flame of hekdesh — no benefit; of non-Jew — permitted. The Gemara asks why and answers: non-Jews avoid it, so the rabbis did not decree; hekdesh people do not avoid, so they decreed. Rashi and Tosafot explain: even though hekdesh people avoid it, opposite a non-Jew it is called not avoided; unlike hullin, opposite which it is avoided. Tosafot: for hekdesh flame is forbidden even if avoided; all the more chametz which is not avoided at all, as Pesachim 18: its flame is forbidden — clear and simple, unlike Bach.",

  "chok-yaakov/2:ג":
    "Forbidden in benefit. See Beit Yosef this siman and Yoreh De'ah 142: he compares chametz to other issurei hana'ah where bread and stew are forbidden only with flame opposite; so Tosafot Pesachim 5a s.v. ve'omer — see there. Nevertheless Acharonim there agree, and here to forbid even by cooking or baking near coals or oven heat; this is also Beit Yosef and the Rav who did not limit here to flame opposite as in Yoreh De'ah 142:4 — see there. Also clear from these Acharonim he has no remedy by casting benefit to the Dead Sea (Magen Avraham: even permitted wood does not help as this-and-that cause — see there). Shach Yoreh De'ah s.k. 8 permits selling to non-Jew except prohibition value, on condition he not resell to Jew; so Shach Yoreh De'ah 110 and Peri Chadash; so poskim imply. Magen Avraham: from Maggid Mishneh \"no remedy\" implies no remedy at all; reason as Mordechai Beitzah 2 on yayin nesech d'oraisa, and so chametz — sale does not help except prohibition value; Tosafot Pesachim 75 implies with flame opposite it is d'oraisa, so sale to non-Jew does not help — end quote. From Maggid Mishneh there is no proof, for he follows Rambam that casting benefit-value and selling except prohibition value are one law, not like us; see Yoreh De'ah Beit Yosef 336. What he cited from Mordechai — he did not examine well: Mordechai only where issur itself is mixed in, unlike here where benefit is only from the issur; see Mordechai's proof from hiring workers: halacha like R' Shimon — barrels in barrels yes, wine in wine no, as Yoreh De'ah 336. Therefore the essence is Shach — his words are clear with compelling proofs; see there. Without flame opposite it is only d'rabbanan; see Tosafot first chapter Pesachim 5.",

  "chok-yaakov/2:ד":
    "His coals — likewise ash. If he betrothed with them, since some hold like R' Yehudah that biur is only burning and all burned items' ash is permitted, it is a doubt in kiddushin — so Acharonim Orach Chayim 22. In any case it appears kiddushin require a get, since coals and ash are only d'rabbanan; even if they resembled chametz d'rabbanan during d'oraisa hours, it is a doubt in kiddushin as Orach Chayim 28 s.k. 31; see siman 443 s.k. 6.",

  "chok-yaakov/2:ה":
    "Forbidden in benefit. Nevertheless permitted to cover slaughter blood, for mitzvot were not given for benefit; likewise permitted to cover with ir ha'nidachat earth in chapter Kisui HaDam; there it implies even l'chatchila Rava permits covering; in such a case he followed Maharam of Rothenburg responsum 100 who forced to reconcile his father's words, but in truth it is explicitly against the Gemara; Levush on shofar of ir ha'nidachat — one should not blow l'chatchila, unlike covering blood as Tosafot there; see siman 455.",

  "chok-yaakov/3:א":
    "He may throw where ravens are found. See Taz who challenges from siman 438 regarding a mouse dragging bread — we do not rely on possible mouse eating to remove certainty. In truth it is simple: there it is a mouse that drags in concealed places needing checking; here one places in an open place before ravens where we assume they will see it — unlike mice that hide. What Taz and Acharonim challenge from siman 433 — I settled there in s.k. 12 — see there.",

  "chok-yaakov/3:ב":
    "That the place is ownerless — Taz and Magen Avraham: not literally ownerless, e.g. placed in public domain where all agree no biur needed since not in his domain; before prohibition time even l'chatchila hefker suffices (Bava Kama 80 — see there). Here it means his courtyard, called hefker meaning released to ravens. Taz challenged Hagahot Maimoniyot brought in Beit Yosef: Hagahot Maimoniyot begins at biur time requiring proper biur, not helping even in hefker place, as Yerushalmi proves; but his end deals with one's courtyard — clearly unlike Bach who explained Hagahot Maimoniyot as partners' courtyard; Taz correctly: partners are obligated like one person. Do not challenge from \"it shall not be seen to you\" — \"to you\" excludes partners, as Chullin 136; Tosafot Sukkah 72: \"you shall destroy leaven from your houses\" includes partners, and \"to you\" excludes hekdesh and others' — as Pesachim.",

  "eshel-avraham/1:א":
    "Or throws it. Like the Sages in chapter 21 — both methods work; Rambam not like Rif. He forbids burning for the Sages because ash is forbidden. Magen Avraham challenged Rambam: why rule to burn — chametz is among buried items whose ash is forbidden? Requires study (Maharil buries coals — see there). Chok Yaakov: chametz is not in \"all buried items shall not be burned\"; also because of \"it shall not be seen\" stringency; also we destroy before prohibition time.",

  "machatzit-hashekel/1:_":
    "(s.k. 1) He burns it, etc. — \"shall not be burned\" — we decreed, etc.; possibly meaning: he shall not burn to treat it like burned items, etc.; we did not decree, etc. What Magen Avraham wrote at the mishnah's end \"shall not be burned lest,\" etc. — is not the mishnah's words but Magen Avraham's reason; therefore one may say the Rambam does not explain the mishnah as a decree, rather the mishnah's intent is not to treat it like burned items.",

  "magen-avraham/1:א":
    "He burns it. In our Gemara: \"and the Sages say, even crumbles,\" etc. — certainly burning is preferable; \"even crumbles\" suffices (Bach). But Rif, Rosh, and Sages' girsa: \"crumbles,\" etc. — implies specifically crumbling; so Tur — for the Sages it is among buried items whose ash is forbidden; we learned end Temurah all buried items shall not be burned lest one benefit from ash. It is astounding the Rambam ruled ash forbidden yet permits burning; end Hilchot Pesulei HaMukdashim he ruled all buried items shall not be burned — though stringent in burning, lenient in ash, for buried ash is forbidden. Possibly: he shall not burn to treat it like burned items; but if they want to burn without benefiting from ash, it is permitted, and they did not decree lest one benefit from ash; so Maharil burns and buries coals.",

  "magen-avraham/1:ב":
    "Hard chametz — such as wheat requires crumbling; so Maggid Mishneh for the Rambam. One must say he holds each grain is cut in two or three parts — he does not hold like Rashi that scattering suffices; if so, why say \"if chametz was hard,\" etc. — the reason is not hardness but being in a sack. Rather as we said; soft bread does not need crumbling. Rabbeinu Yehudah explains soft bread needs crumbling, wheat does not — see there. In my humble opinion one should be stringent and crumble both. If throwing into privy, no crumbling; no concern a dog or pig will eat it — if so, fish in water would eat too; we are not concerned since he does not place it for eating. If collected water with fish, forbidden to throw — he benefits. Forbidden in all melachot when burning time arrives until he burns, as siman 431.",

  "magen-avraham/2:ב":
    "Forbidden in benefit — because there is enhancement of wood in bread (Ran, Maggid Mishneh), specifically with flame opposite (Rambam, Kesef Mishneh); and even though chametz coals and ash are forbidden, nevertheless it is this-and-that cause — the oven also causes baking and is permitted; but if he cooked stew, even on ash or coals it is forbidden. Yoreh De'ah 142 requires study. In my humble opinion the essence is Taz and Shach: Rambam and poskim hold orlah — even cooking on coals or in oven heat is forbidden, not for their reasons but Pesachim 26b: even rabbis permit only with flame opposite in orlah because nullified in 200, therefore not stringent; hekdesh not nullified even in 1000 — forbidden; so glowing coals forbidden. Tur did not distinguish new vs. old oven because Rambam in Yoreh De'ah: since orlah not nullified, even this-and-that cause forbidden — so chametz, any benefit. Even if other wood were on the fire and chametz also — this-and-that cause — still forbidden; impossible to kindle chametz alone without wood; Tur wrote bread forbidden — see Yoreh De'ah 142; Maharil: his coals and ash forbidden, even nullification does not help; unlike palm branches fallen on Yom Tov where nullification helps, siman 497:2 — there wood is not food; even if some goes to food, issur is not through its benefit — end quote. Tosafot Shabbat 25b s.v. ha bechol: oil, meat, grain weigh more at kindling — see there.",

  "magen-avraham/2:ג":
    "Forbidden in benefit — and it has no remedy; so the essence (Maggid Mishneh). Meaning: no remedy by casting benefit to the Dead Sea — only for orlah it helps that value attaches and has redemption; see Shach Yoreh De'ah 110 s.k. 2. When that bread mixed with others they say cast benefit; when distinct, not like Levush who is lenient on Dead Sea. Shach permits selling to non-Jew except prohibition value; Yoreh De'ah 142. From Maggid Mishneh \"no remedy\" implies no remedy at all; reason as Mordechai Beitzah 2: moisture around grain is chametz, yayin nesech is d'oraisa, forbids mixture, sale does not help except nesech value; so chametz — end quote. Tosafot Pesachim 75: with flame opposite forbidden d'oraisa, so no sale to non-Jew; Ran and Machatzit HaShekel chapter 2; Shulchan Aruch Yoreh De'ah: no remedy for that bread; possibly with great loss one may be lenient, especially this-and-that cause; some say chametz has redemption via orlah — see Mordechai chapter 3 and Ran chapter 5.",

  "magen-avraham/3:_":
    "Where ravens are found, etc. With this the courtyard need-not-check (Hagahot Maimoniyot) is understood; if in ownerless courtyard, what checking — even chametz, if he declared hefker before six, permitted (Tur 437, Beit Yosef 448 Yerushalmi, Darkei Moshe in name of Or Zaru'a: what difference giving to non-Jew or hefker). Rather it must not be ownerless; what Shulchan Aruch concludes \"even though in ownerless place\" means: since placed in his courtyard, it is hefker for whoever will take, nevertheless since in his domain he must destroy as required — burning for R' Yehudah, as Hagahot Maimoniyot. Kesef Mishneh: those who place chametz on Rosh Hashanah sides at six improperly — once six arrives must remove from the world; Hagahot Maimoniyot — see siman 433 and Yoreh De'ah 446.",

  "mechaber/1:main":
    "The law of destroying chametz, containing 3 seifim: How is chametz destroyed? He burns it, or crumbles and casts to the wind, or throws it into the sea. If the chametz was hard and the sea does not break it quickly, he crumbles it and then throws it into the sea. {Rama: The custom is to burn it; it is good to burn by day, similar to notar which was burned by day (his own opinion). But if he wants to burn immediately after bedikah so a rodent not drag it, the choice is in his hand (Hagahot Maimoniyot chapter 3 and Kol Bo).}",

  "mishnah-berurah/1:ו":
    "(6) And the custom to burn it — we are concerned for poskim who ruled like R' Yehudah that biur of chametz is only burning, derived from notar which is by burning. This custom applies even if burned at the proper time — end of the fifth hour as our custom (siman 434), or any time of the sixth, and all the more if chametz is found after six or on Pesach itself — certainly l'chatchila destroy only by burning.",

  "mishnah-berurah/2:א":
    "(9) To a non-Jew before, etc. — forbidden to give to his gentile male or female slave whose sustenance is on him if the chametz is abundant and cannot be eaten before prohibition time (Chok Yaakov in name of Bach; Chamed Moshe disagrees: we do not care that sustenance is on him since he does not pay at prohibition time but before Pesach; when Pesach comes he owes no more sustenance — only forbidden to say explicitly he should keep chametz all Pesach, for that is wanting chametz to exist; give in stam and say: take this chametz for your debt and you have no sustenance from me all Pesach. So Maharshak in his novellae; at minimum be careful the slave not eat chametz in his house lest they suspect paying debt with chametz, as siman 450:6 in Mishnah Berurah.)",

  "mishnah-berurah/2:ג":
    "(11) To benefit from its coals, etc. — chametz was not yet forbidden in benefit; likewise permitted in the fifth hour to cook and other benefits. Nevertheless the custom is to make a separate bonfire even in the fifth hour so they not come to be lenient at the sixth; they burn in the courtyard, not the stove [Acharonim].",

  "mishnah-berurah/2:ה":
    "(13) In benefit — even if the bread mixed with other loaves and is unrecognizable, all are forbidden in benefit; no remedy to permit via redeeming benefit-value cast to destruction [Yoreh De'ah 110 Shach s.k. 2, 142]. Acharonim: there is remedy to sell bread to non-Jew except prohibition value — deduct benefit-value of chametz used to kindle from price paid to non-Jew, so he does not benefit from chametz kindling; crumble bread before selling [where pat akum permitted] lest he resell to Jew. Even if not mixed, this sale remedy exists; some forbid if not mixed; with great loss or pressing need, rely to be lenient.",

  "mishnah-berurah/2:ו":
    "(14) And likewise the coals — likewise ash; therefore forbidden to warm by coals or benefit from their light, etc.; likewise forbidden to launder with ash, etc. Nevertheless permitted to cover slaughter blood, for mitzvot were not given for benefit [Acharonim].",

  "mishnah-berurah/3:ב":
    "(17) Where ravens are found — we rely they will eat it; see siman 433:6 where we cited many Acharonim: this law is only in a public ownerless street, not one's courtyard.",

  "mishnah-berurah/3:ג":
    "(18) Rather he shall destroy it — Acharonim disagree: if he declared hefker and threw to ownerless place before prohibition, no further biur obligation (siman 433). Some explain Mechaber also holds so, but deals with another case (Magen Avraham, Chok Yaakov, Sha'arei Efraim). Even if placed in ownerless place, he must declare complete hefker in speech and heart, not intending to reclaim after Pesach — otherwise not complete hefker while not yet acquired by another.",

  "peri-megadim/1:א":
    "Or — Taz: his holy words need explanation. Mechaber's wording is Rambam chapter 3 law 11; Maggid Mishneh ruled like R' Yosef; so Kesef Mishneh there. He explains wheat and bread opposite Rashi 28a s.v. bechitei — scattering from sack; soft bread sometimes needs crumbling. Rambam: wheat need not crumble even if gathered (except in sack) — scatter; hard bread sometimes needs crumbling. \"If hard and sea does not cut quickly\" — if wheat required grinding, soft bread would not be included; he wrote stam, should have said wheat specifically — opposite Rashi. In Laws of Idolatry 8:6 how a non-Jew destroys — grind and cast to wind or burn and cast to Dead Sea, like R' Yosef — end quote there. I thought Rambam's \"how to destroy — burn or crumble and cast\" means l'chatchila burn and cast so ash not food and no this-and-that cause; crumble also for one not meticulous like R' Yosi in Avodah Zarah 43b — this-and-that cause permitted; for non-Jew the opposite — wood and stone ground do not grow, burned grows, see Chullin 88 Tosafot s.v. shechikat. Darkei Moshe: l'chatchila best grind and cast without burning — no growth, no this-and-that cause; burning best to Dead Sea — end quote. If so, why not write for the less meticulous he may burn and cast? Understand; see there. And if so, why write stam — should say wheat specifically? Opposite Rashi entirely. In ch. 8 idolatry 6 how non-Jew destroys — grind, cast, or burn and cast to sea like R' Yosef. I thought Mechaber means l'chatchila burn/cast; crumble for less meticulous; burning to sea best. If so, why not write less meticulous may burn/cast? Understand; see there and in ch. 3 Orach Chayim law 11 he elaborated four disputes between Rabbah and R' Yosef.",

  "peri-megadim/1:ב":
    "And behold Taz explains Rambam like Rabbah — where no gentiles, even metals need not grinding; chametz in sea needs no crumbling even with gentiles — water cuts fast. Other rivers need crumbling. R' Yosef: no repugnance in water; chametz is repugnant in water though not cut — people won't take it (Rashi: root maas; Rambam per Taz: root maas). If explaining like Rashi, wheat and bread dissolve — Rambam would follow R' Yosef. In truth halacha follows Rabbah over three; Rambam should explain accordingly; Taz: Rambam explains like R' Yosef because of repugnance — cannot explain like Rashi lest one think he rules R' Yosef; must say Rambam takes leniency Rabbah did not mention — if not hard, no crumbling. Tur is clearer — rules Rabbah, does not distinguish Dead Sea from rivers; Beit Yosef: stringent where we are not expert which waters are strong — distant from us. Taz Yoreh De'ah 146 note 11.",

  "peri-megadim/2:_":
    "And the custom — Taz: if he fulfilled burning in the fifth hour and later finds chametz, destroy like any chametz during Pesach; if found on Yom Tov, break vessel and bury it; burn even though he already fulfilled burning, for R' Yehudah's burning may be d'oraisa on Pesach like notar — karet and \"you shall not leave\"; chametz likewise. Erev Pesach until night — no karet, only \"it shall not be seen\"; therefore lenient also on the 14th and other stringencies since no karet until night (siman 447). If so, at six we burn from custom like R' Yehudah per Rashi; unlike when already fulfilled burning — see note 3. Tosafot Pesachim 12b s.v. eimatai — for Rabbenu Tam R' Yehudah burns from seven too; Tosafot 28a s.v. vechametz, 27b s.v. ein — requires study. Know we rule halacha like Sages who bury and ash forbidden — not a doubt R' Yehudah vs. Sages, rather both stringencies; practical difference: doubt if matzah baked on chametz coals — perhaps certainly Sages, and in Pri Megadim Yoreh De'ah we wrote doubt of dispute, mixed forbidden (see there). Beit Shmuel Even HaEzer 28:54 and Choshen Mishpat there imply doubt R' Yehudah vs. Sages. Beit Shmuel: she may sell to a gentile — requires study, for to him it is forbidden; see Even HaEzer laws of marriage — requires study whether ash is definite issur or doubt.",

  "peri-megadim/3:_":
    "And if — Taz: his words are hard to understand. If we explain \"from the sixth hour\" for R' Yehudah — when he has wood, mitzvah is burning at six; if no wood, he must not wait until seven lest he violate positive or negative mitzvah momentarily — all the more if he destroys from six so as not to violate \"you shall not see\" as Taz wrote. Or: only regarding going out to the army is there leniency when no wood now — if he destroys in all ways now, called later idle? Torah said destroy — applies to army too. Difference: at five or six without wood, destroying general biur for \"you shall destroy\" that applies that day — not called later idle; unlike army where no obligation yet if no wood for complete destruction as R' Yehudah — if he destroys other ways he is idle from burning mitzvah; if no wood now, idle; leniency vs. army. Rashi's challenge from army: for R' Yehudah no obligation to destroy all ways when no wood — if he destroys other ways, idle from burning; if no wood, idle — leniency. Rosh: at five he may destroy all ways — not idle, already removed from world, nothing to burn; Tur's questions resolved. Practical difference: one who checks and burns per custom — perhaps halacha like R' Yehudah; nullify at night in bedikah; by day when burning he does not nullify until Pesach (siman 436 Mishnah Berurah 1, 444 Mishnah Berurah note 3); see Pesachim novellae.",

  "shaarei-teshuvah/1:_":
    "In Shulchan Aruch seif 1 \"or crumbles,\" etc. — see responsum Or Yisrael who elaborated poskim' dispute whether biur is only burning vs. nullification alone; see Sha'agat Aryeh chapters 83, 87; see chapter 85 that women are obligated in positive mitzvah \"you shall destroy,\" even regarding the obligation of destruction from six and onward — see there.",

  "shaarei-teshuvah/2:_":
    "In Shulchan Aruch seif 3 \"and if he finds after prohibition,\" etc. — responsum Pri Chadash 12: one had chametz in a gentile's house, forgot to sell; after chatzot remembered and nullified before chatzot as customary; asked what to do — advised to leave in ownerless place for dogs or pigs; if gentiles acquire it, buy cheap after Pesach or they return it — gentile in whose house chametz is will not let dogs waste it, he acquires and may return; see there he elaborated Gemara Yerushalmi in name of Rivash, source of this law; see Acharonim siman 433:6.",

  "turei-zahav/1:א":
    "Or crumbles, etc. Rabbah holds chametz needs crumbling even for throwing to sea; distinguishes Dead Sea — no ships, no crumbling — unlike other rivers. R' Yehudah: chametz needs no crumbling since repugnant; baraita needing crumbling — wheat vs. bread per Rashi: fermented wheat in sack — scatter on water; Rosh ruled Rabbah; Shulchan Aruch here is Rambam's wording — implies R' Yosef distinguishing wheat and bread; \"if chametz hard,\" etc. Why rule R' Yehudah when we rule Rabbah over three? Tur did not distinguish hard chametz — holds Rabbah. Rambam also — difference Dead Sea vs. rivers not per Rashi but Dead Sea's cold salty water cuts fast. R' Yosef's wheat/bread distinction — Rambam per repugnance: lenient only for bread that repulses in water; wheat can be rinsed. Rambam rules Rabbah — Dead Sea vs. rivers from water strength; Rambam omitted wheat/bread — only R' Yehudah. Now Tur's omission of Dead Sea distinction is fine — we do not decide; be stringent in all waters requiring crumbling — so appears to me, though Maggid Mishneh wrote Rambam like R' Yehudah — my soul is divided; he is correct in my humble opinion.",

  "turei-zahav/1:ב":
    "Custom to burn, etc. Reason for burning: dispute R' Yehudah vs. Sages on burning; many poskim dispute — act stringently. One who fulfills burning at six and later finds pieces need not burn — destroy from the world; from six onward we are lenient on chametz like other issurim — insignificant amount, notar for damage (siman 447); even on Pesach we rule Sages except custom like R' Yehudah; here before night, already fulfilled burning at six — no need to burn again.",

  "turei-zahav/2:א":
    "And if he burned before the sixth hour, etc. Gemara 12b: R' Yehudah — when not at biur time; at biur time destroy in all ways. Rashi: not at biur time — beginning of six, still d'oraisa permitted; at biur time — seven, etc. Tur brought Rosh: Rashi at five hour destroy all ways since permitted in benefit. Tur challenged from Rashi 27b on \"did not find wood\" — sit idle? Rashi: for R' Yehudah at biur time destroy all ways; leniency for army — must destroy, no wood — sit idle. Bach wanted to settle — see there. In my humble opinion Rashi does not challenge Rosh: why mention army — challenge from six when no wood should sit idle? Must be: for R' Yehudah at biur time burning is essential; general biur obligation remains without wood — destroy other ways or later before night; not idle. Army: no obligation until biur time — if no wood for R' Yehudah's burning, if he destroys other ways he is idle from burning mitzvah; no wood — idle — leniency. Rosh at five may destroy all ways — not idle, already biured; nothing to burn; Tur's questions fall away.",

  "turei-zahav/2:ב":
    "And likewise the coals, etc. Meaning from the chametz itself, not the wood. Tur: even after extinguished per Rambam biur chametz — all buried items ash forbidden; even no chametz coals, mere ash forbidden for Rambam; \"coals\" here is extra.",

  "turei-zahav/3:_":
    "Before its prohibition time, etc. I did not understand the Rav: Hagahot Maimoniyot brought in Beit Yosef — Rivash forbade throwing chametz; proof Yerushalmi: do not feed chametz even to other dogs; before prohibition may throw where ravens found since not yet forbidden in benefit; if found after prohibition do not leave for ravens — essence of biur is burning — end quote. In next siman he will write Rashi's view. What is written here \"if found after prohibition if ravens did not eat\" — if meaning found in house after prohibition, cannot leave in ravens' place as biur — biur is only burning (Beit Yosef next siman per Tur: removing from domain suffices after prohibition, unlike Hagahot Maimoniyot earlier). This gloss also means he removes to ownerless for ravens — Hagahot Maimoniyot contradicts Shulchan Aruch here \"if ravens did not eat\" — implies he threw before prohibition; if found that chametz there, must take and destroy — contradicts next siman. If Shulchan Aruch means ownerless for ravens, like giving to non-Jew before prohibition — why must reclaim after prohibition? Levush: even if hefker'd, not in others' domain — must take and destroy. This fits Shulchan Aruch but contradicts Beit Yosef next siman. If Hagahot Maimoniyot beginning also means ravens eat in his domain, permitted before prohibition — contradicts Tur 438 mouse — certainty not removed by doubt ravens ate; here permitted from \"perhaps ravens will eat\"? Beit Yosef 433 certainly chametz needs checking even before prohibition; Rama challenged there — did not decide; essence: Shulchan Aruch certainly requires checking even before prohibition; here Hagahot Maimoniyot not lenient from mouse-eating — forbidden even before prohibition unless he stands so they eat before prohibition. Hagahot Maimoniyot: throw to true ownerless even near prohibition time — certainly ravens won't eat before prohibition — no benefit prohibition even to other dogs — that applies after prohibition when placing before them; end: if found after time — in house after prohibition cannot exempt biur because ravens will take in ownerless place; even at rabbinic prohibition hour five, definite chametz in his domain forbidden even before prohibition lest remain after prohibition. Mahari Weil explained Hagahot Maimoniyot siman 433: distinguish shared courtyard vs. dedicated courtyard; Taz: partners obligated like one person. Do not challenge \"it shall not be seen to you\" excludes partners, Chullin 136; Tosafot Sukkah 72: \"from your houses\" includes partners; \"to you\" excludes hekdesh and others — Pesachim.",

  "yad-ephraim/2:_":
    "There s.k. 3 — Gemara 12b: \"when\" — Taz s.k. 7 is hard to understand. Plain reason: before prohibition he may destroy as he wishes; Rosh: Tur holds R' Yehudah requires fulfilling both destruction mitzvot by burning lest called idle. Forced from Rashi: rabbis challenge R' Yehudah from army. Taz reconciles: plain challenge should be from six — if no wood, sit idle — great leniency — at seven he destroys all ways, perhaps moment passes violating \"you shall destroy.\" Taz: if destroys from six to avoid \"you shall not see\" — not called later idle even without burning mitzvah. Only army: no obligation to destroy all ways when no wood — if destroys other ways, idle from burning; no wood — idle — leniency. Rashi's challenge only army; Rosh at five destroying all ways not idle; army: if destroys now, idle from burning when no wood; at five/six without wood destroying general biur for \"you shall destroy\" that day — not idle; unlike army before obligation. I labored days ago — forced; now saw Acharonim also forced; his intent is clear — I summarize. Gemara: R' Yehudah learned chametz by burning from kal va'chomer; they said every din you judge beginning stringent ending lenient — \"did not find wood\" — sit idle? Rashi: leniency for army. Tur challenged Rosh who permits destroying all ways before prohibition. Taz: per Tur's understanding of Rashi, army traveler not fulfilling burning mitzvah is called idle since biur obligation applies and no wood — should challenge from six: no wood at six for R' Yehudah's burning — sit idle? Rashi needed army case. Answer: even without wood he must biur before night other ways or later — not idle. Army: for R' Yehudah no obligation to destroy all ways without wood — destroys other ways = idle from burning; no wood = idle — leniency. Chachamim obligate destroying all ways even without wood. They told R' Yehudah: you will sit idle — for you, no obligation to destroy without wood; for us, obligation — Torah said destroy. Three matters: (1) army before six — (2) in house before prohibition — (3) after prohibition from six. No leniency at six without wood for either — must destroy lest violate bal yera'eh. At five or before, no destruction obligation yet — may destroy all ways even for R' Yehudah. Only army: if no wood, R' Yehudah need not destroy at all — may sit idle relying on verbal nullification; Chachamim must destroy all ways. Hence kal va'chomer ends lenient for army only. Tur's challenge on Rosh fails; at five/six destroying all ways not idle; army if destroys now idle from burning; R' Yehudah without wood need not destroy — may sit idle — leniency. Rosh: before prohibition may destroy all ways — not idle; nothing to burn later; army: Chachamim obligate destruction — R' Yehudah without wood may sit idle — leniency. Three contexts explained — Taz's words are correct.",

  "yad-ephraim/3:_":
    "Magen Avraham s.k. 5 — Rambam and poskim: orlah — even this-and-that cause, oil/meat/grain weigh more at kindling — explain his words: specifically with flame opposite — though oven also causes, wood enhancement recognizable in bread, oven enhancement less/nullified — unlike coals/ash where enhancement not recognizable, like oven alone; since this-and-that cause permitted. Cooking on coals alone — one cause — forbidden. Magen Avraham: essence like Shach — orlah even on glowing coals or oven heat forbidden; Gemara: orlah nullified in 200, hekdesh not in 1000 — rabbis permit only flame opposite; so glowing coals forbidden. Tur did not distinguish new/old oven — should permit old oven on glowing coals? Tur stam forbids for rabbis — reason: since not nullified, this-and-that cause does not help like hekdesh and orlah. Difference from Shach: per Shach, if chametz and permitted wood, could permit — both enhancements visible; per this reason forbidden even with permitted wood since chametz/orlah not nullified (Shach may agree enhancement of forbidden wood is as in bread, permitted wood does not help). Maharil: coals/ash do not nullify — unlike palm branches Yom Tov 497:2 — not food; chametz has redemption — still not nullified by burning. Not like branches where prohibition consumed — chametz is food, prohibition comes to food through benefit, not nullified like non-food. Tur should have distinguished old oven on glowing coals — Tur forbids rabbis always. Practical: if permitted wood with chametz wood, Shach might permit; this reason forbids always. Maharil: even coals no nullification — unlike mukztah branches; chametz has redemption; prohibition not consumed — food benefit not nullified. Tosafot: oil/meat/grain significant at kindling — if other wood with chametz, does not help (unless also grain/matzah at kindling). Coals already formed — requires study; Tosafot significant only at kindling; see Magen Avraham 497:2, 325:9 on mukztah wood.",

  "eliyah-rabbah/1:_":
    "[1] And if the chametz was hard, etc. Magen Avraham was stringent that even wheat requires crumbling — cutting each grain in three parts — as the Rambam and Chok Yaakov rejected his view. In my humble opinion the essence is Magen Avraham, as clear from Rambam's explanation on the mishnah beginning chapter Kol Sha'ah; also in the Rif there: \"wheat of chametz requires grinding and then throwing to the sea\" — end quote. Although Beit Chadash implies it is not from the Rif, it is a gloss of a great authority — but specifically dry wheat. Magen Avraham: hard chametz thrown into privy needs no crumbling — no concern dog or pig will eat since not placed for eating; if he has collected water with fish, forbidden to throw — he benefits.",

  "eliyah-rabbah/3:_":
    "[3] And if he finds it, etc. — and Acharonim disagree; if he declared hefker and threw to ownerless place before prohibition, no further biur (siman 433). Some explain Mechaber also holds so but another case (Magen Avraham, Chok Yaakov, Sha'arei Efraim). Even in ownerless place, complete hefker in speech and heart required, not intending to reclaim after Pesach.",

  "chokhmat-shlomo/1:_":
    "Seif 1 — How to destroy chametz: burn or crumble, etc. Note: see Magen Avraham s.k. 1 who cites Rambam end Hilchot Pesulei HaMukdashim: all buried items shall not be burned — though stringent in burning, lenient in ash, etc. I questioned Pesachim 27: why did the Sages say to R' Yehudah every din beginning stringent ending lenient is not a din — if no wood to burn, etc., better to say: stringent in burning, lenient in ash, ending lenient? Possibly they said so to R' Yehudah per his view end Temurah: if he wants to be stringent and burn, he may; he does not consider leniency in ash a leniency, or holds ash of burned items is forbidden — therefore for him it is not \"ending lenient\" from this. Better: at biur time \"you shall destroy\" is in all ways; for R' Yehudah it is among burned items so ash permitted — leniency; for rabbis among buried, ash forbidden — only leniency in burning before biur when even buried ash permitted. They said: if you judge before biur, when even buried ash is permitted, if no wood he sits idle — necessarily ending lenient. Burning is stringent for even buried then ash permitted. See Ohr Chadash on my question; Pri Megadim siman 449 note 1 seems like my view. Further question on Gemara: R' Yehudah returned and judged another din from notar — chametz lighter than notar? Notar is lav linked to aseh only when violating lav; leaving first to fulfill aseh forbidden — but Tosafot Pesachim 29: holding chametz to destroy does not violate lav — proves one may violate lav initially to fulfill aseh; chametz lighter than notar — why proof? Requires study. A scholar asked: proof from kodshim to hullin? Kodshim stricter. It fits: we learn leniency from kodshim as Taz Yoreh De'ah 3 — kal va'chomer hullin from kodshim; R' Yehudah: if burning stringent, kal va'chomer from kodshim; what will they answer \"ending lenient\"? If burning lenient, still learn leniency hullin from kodshim as Taz — study well. (Same seif) Note Magen Avraham s.k. 2: chametz is not called communal positive mitzvah; see Hilchot Megillah 696:7 — meat and wine on Purim are communal aseh; opposite Magen Avraham here; see my novellae Pesachim 92 and Or LaChayim 5681 41a. My resolution: Shulchan Aruch Megillah why not write opposite — communal aseh of Purim overrides rabbinic aninut of individual, or aseh overriding aseh; when mitzvah needs intent (or stam intent), intentional for mitzvah — aseh overrides lav or stricter aseh; when act permitted without intent but forbidden by another aseh/lav blocking — blocking aseh overrides blocked aseh; Purim eating without intent is mitzvah, only blocked by aninut — aninut communal somewhat like Torah — cannot override; Megillah wording \"permitted in meat and wine\" — forbidding would be aninut overriding Purim aseh, impossible; chametz needs intent for biur, cannot do on Shabbat — Shabbat aseh overrides chametz aseh; chametz not true communal aseh since individual does not discharge public; for aseh-docheh-aseh need great stringency — Purim not enough; Megillah precise wording explains Magen Avraham; resolves my Pesachim question why not Purim aseh overrides aninut on Pesach — Pesach needs intent, not communal aseh, aninut stronger; see my work.",

  "chokhmat-shlomo/2:_":
    "(Same seif 2) And if he gave to a non-Jew before the sixth hour, he need not destroy, etc. Note: I saw Chok Yaakov and Bach citing Bach responsum: forbidden to give to worker or gentile servant if abundant chametz — like paying debt with issurei hana'ah — see there. Their words are very surprising: paying debt with issurei hana'ah is when at payment time it is forbidden in benefit; if permitted at payment, what matter that later forbidden — may one not pay gentile debt in chametz even before due? So too here: gentile receives and is released from sustenance before prohibition; if sold to gentile who owes, permitted — here like sale to gentile, gentile owes wages, pays with chametz, releases sustenance — permitted as siman 450. Also siman 450 permits telling grocer to give worker chametz and employer pays — not paying debt with issurei hana'ah (Magen Avraham); even stringent agree when paying before Pesach. Therefore any acquisition valid generally works here; not on Jew's responsibility if lost — must feed more — same if little chametz remains; like accepting responsibility on gentile's chametz in gentile's domain — permitted. Why is worker permitted dinar for chametz — even when chametz comes to worker, Jew responsible if lost — like responsibility on gentile chametz — permitted. Main point as my novellae: chametz responsibility not forbidden; he wants it to exist only if obligation stems from chametz itself; not when obligation elsewhere, chametz only releases debt — not responsibility, not wanting existence — like scaring lion (Bava Kama) — not benefit; paying debt permitted knowingly only if giving in hand after prohibition; telling grocer or giving in hand while still permitted — not he who gives later, mere benefit — not worse than paying debt unknowingly — scaring lion. Bach's words surprising. In my humble opinion Bach deals only when gentile eats in Jew's house — Agudah brought by Magen Avraham siman 450: do not eat in his house — suspicion of paying debt with issurei hana'ah; Bach's intent: suspicion they say he pays debt with issurei hana'ah at Pesach. If not in Jew's house, certainly permitted; only in Jew's house forbidden lest suspicion. Why not mention transgressing bal yera'ah — since nullification suffices, presumably nullified, won't leave permitted and eat forbidden; rabbinic prohibition permitted in inner rooms (Magen Avraham 431). Bach cited paying-debt reason — Torah prohibition, forbidden even inner rooms. Without this, Bach's words have no explanation; for law, seems full permission except not in his house per Agudah. Later I received Bach responsa — not siman 147 as Chok Yaakov said but 143; Bach follows stringent view siman 450; for lenient view certainly permitted here. Chok Yaakov surprising to cite in stam; Bach's text surprising — cites Agudah forbidding dinar for chametz — there at prohibition time; here still permitted time — can one not pay before due if creditor agrees to release debt? Without proof I bring Gemara Pesachim: Rabbah Gamliel betrothed with Kordyene wheat from six onward — no concern for kiddushin. Why not teach greater leniency — betrothal before permitted time, kiddushin not effective until six when chametz forbidden, especially according to those who hold after six d'oraisa? Must be Rabbah's view kiddushin effective — Ran: money consumed with her permission; here chametz already hers, only debt remains — kiddushin in debt permitted, not in chametz though chametz visible — like not in domain. All the more giving worker chametz in permitted time — not benefiting from issurei hana'ah. Do not rely on Bach here — study. (Same seif) But if burned from sixth hour, forbidden in benefit, shall not kindle oven, etc. Note: incident — woman threw challah on Shabbat HaGadol before Pesach into fire thinking it burned; coals swept from oven left for Pesach, used to cook — challah found in coals. I permitted stews except pot adjacent to challah likely touched — forbade that. Reason: charred before time permitted after — if known well charred, no concern; unknown — concern not charred well (Rosh on morayis); \"fit for dog\" only when distinct, but by mixture if unfit for human eating, nullified; this-and-that cause permitted in issurei hana'ah by nullification — chametz by mixture; Rosh feared not charred well — he holds mixing intentionally like distinct; for spontaneous mixture, if unfit for dog suffices. Tur permits morayis in benefit — lenient, no concern not charred well; especially some hold this-and-that cause permitted in chametz — rely in pressing case. Concern taste from challah on adjacent pot — not this-and-that cause — Magen Avraham forbids even benefit — I forbade that dish, permitted others. Because in this siman I wrote extensively in my works on this-and-that cause and \"in 1000 not nullified\" even when this-and-that cause forbidden, I excerpt thoughts on Rambam Temurah ch. 3:5 — blemished fetus of kodshim, redeemed, then exchanged — temurah not offered. Lechem Mishneh wondered: Bekhorot — if conceived before redemption, born after, calf permitted; if conceived after redemption, forbidden; fetus stricter than temurah — fetus partners in kodsh, temurah not. In my humble opinion: even this-and-that cause permitted, hekdesh not nullified in 1000 — calf after redemption conceived is this-and-that cause; before redemption not this-and-that cause — redemption not yet complete, not this-and-that cause. No fetus of offering — temurah of offering exists — forbidden. Kereshut fetus stricter than temurah; where this-and-that cause, not considered fetus of offering. Fits. Question whether temurah lashes — study. On Magen Avraham: where insignificant forbidden, even this-and-that cause forbidden — see my Avodah Zarah 68 on \"in 1000\" — study.",
};
